\begin_layout Title
Econometrics I: Quiz 6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a linear regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta+u$
\end_inset

, which satisfies the Gauss-Markov assumptions except for the condition:
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset

.
 Instead, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}\Omega$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\neq I$
\end_inset

.
 The OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a linear unbiased estimator, but it is not the best.
 Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is known, derive the BLUE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}=P^{\prime}P$
\end_inset

.
 Pre-multiply the regression model by 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y^{*}=X^{*}\beta+u^{*}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}=Py$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{*}=PX$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{*}=Pu$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the BLUE is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=(X^{*\prime}X^{*})^{-1}X^{*\prime}y^{*}=(X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}y
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is BLUE because
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u^{*})=Pcov(u)P^{\prime}=\sigma^{2}P(P^{\prime}P)^{-1}P^{\prime}=\sigma^{2}PP^{-1}P^{\prime-1}P^{\prime}=\sigma^{2}I
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a system of two equations, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}=X_{j}\beta_{j}+u_{j},\ j=1,2$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of dependent variables, and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times K_{j}$
\end_inset

 matrix of exogenous variables.
 The error terms have the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{j})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{j}u_{k}^{\prime})=\sigma_{jk}I$
\end_inset

.
 Each error term satisfies the Gauss-Markov assumptions, and they are 
\emph on
contemporaneously 
\emph default
correlated.
 Show that the GLS estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 are identical to their individual OLS estimators if 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}\equiv X_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Write the model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
y_{2}
\end{array}\right] & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
X_{1} & 0\\
0 & X_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{1}\\
\beta_{2}
\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{c}
u_{1}\\
u_{2}
\end{array}\right]\\
y & = & X\beta+u
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Omega=E(uu^{\prime})=E\left\{ \left[\begin{array}{c}
u_{1}\\
u_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
u_{1} & u_{2}\end{array}\right]\right\} =E\left[\begin{array}{cc}
u_{1}u_{1}^{\prime} & u_{1}u_{2}^{\prime}\\
u_{2}u_{1}^{\prime} & u_{2}u_{2}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma_{11}I & \sigma_{12}I\\
\sigma_{21}I & \sigma_{22}I
\end{array}\right]=\Sigma\otimes I
\]

\end_inset

Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GLS}=\left(X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}y
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z=X_{1}=X_{2}$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $X=I\otimes Z$
\end_inset

.
 Then, using the relationships 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\otimes B)^{\prime}=(A^{\prime}\otimes B^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\otimes B)(C\otimes D)=(AC\otimes BD)$
\end_inset

, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{GLS} & = & \left[X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)X{}^{-1}\right]X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y\\
 & = & \left[\left(I\otimes Z^{\prime}\right)(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)(I\otimes Z)\right]^{-1}\left(I\otimes Z^{\prime}\right)(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y\\
 & = & \left[\Sigma^{-1}\otimes Z^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes Z^{\prime})y\\
 & = & [I\otimes(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}]y\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime} & 0\\
0 & (Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
y_{2}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y_{1}\\
(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y_{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
The random individual effect model of a panel data specifies the model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{ti}=\alpha_{i}+X_{ti}\beta+u_{ti}=\alpha+X_{ti}\beta+(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti})=\alpha+X_{ti}\beta+e_{ti}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}=\alpha+\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 A standard set of assumptions includes that 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ti}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\epsilon_{i},u_{tj})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i,j,t$
\end_inset

.
 Under these assumptions, derive the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ti}$
\end_inset

 and covariances of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ti}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{sj}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
First, we find the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ti}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Var(e_{ti}) & = & Var(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti})\\
 & = & Var(\epsilon_{i})+Var(u_{ti})\\
 & = & \sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The covariance of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ti}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{sj}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(e_{ti},e_{sj}) & = & cov\left[\left(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti}\right),\left(\epsilon_{j}+u_{sj}\right)\right]\\
 & = & E\left\{ \left[\left(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti}\right)-E\left(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti}\right)\right]\left[\left(\epsilon_{j}+u_{sj}\right)-E\left(\epsilon_{j}+u_{sj}\right)\right]\right\} \\
 & = & E\left[\left(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti}\right)\left(\epsilon_{j}+u_{sj}\right)\right]\\
 & = & E\left(\epsilon_{i}\epsilon_{j}\right)+E\left(u_{ti}u_{sj}\right)\\
cov(e_{ti},e_{sj}) & = & \begin{cases}
\sigma_{u}^{2}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2} & \mbox{if}\ t=s,\ i=j\\
\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2} & \mbox{if}\ t\neq s,\ i=j\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
The ARCH(1) model specifies the error term process as 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=\sigma_{t}\epsilon_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{t}^{2}=\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}u_{t-1}^{2},\ \epsilon_{t}\sim i.i.d.(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{0}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $1>\alpha_{1}\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Verify that OLS estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the BLUE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{t-s},\ \forall\ s\geq1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{t}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\epsilon_{t})=0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{t})=E(\sigma_{t}\epsilon_{t})=E(\sigma_{t})E(\epsilon_{t})=0
\]

\end_inset

Also, since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\epsilon_{t}^{2})=1$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{t}^{2})=E(\sigma_{t}^{2}\epsilon_{t}^{2})=E(\sigma_{t}^{2})E(\epsilon_{t}^{2})=E(\sigma_{t}^{2})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
The observed dependent variable in a binary choice model takes two values:
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the major problem with estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

 by the OLS?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The binary choice model is often motivated by the latent variable model.
 What is it and how is it related to the observed values of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=1$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The marginal effect on the probability of choice, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1)$
\end_inset

, is not a linear funciton of the coefficient.
 Show the marginal effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ik}$
\end_inset

 and explain an important factor to be considered in measuring it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Present how you use the MLE to estimate the model.
 (Not the final solution, but just the specification of the objective function).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
The major problem with estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

 by the OLS is that the predicted values 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 may not be in the unit interval.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i1}=Z_{i1}\beta+\epsilon_{i1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i0}=Z_{i0}\beta+\epsilon_{i1}$
\end_inset

 represent the utility choice 1 and choice 0 brings to agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 The latent variable model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}^{*}=U_{1i}-U_{i0}=(Z_{i1}-Z_{i0})\beta+(\epsilon_{i1}-\epsilon_{i0})\equiv X_{i}\beta+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

However, we do not observe the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, only its sign.
 Thus, the binary response model is specified as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}>0\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the pdf is symmetric, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial F(X_{i}\beta)}{\partial X_{ik}}=f(X_{i}\beta)\beta_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To find the MLE, we wish to maximize the following function with respect
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & {\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n}}F(X_{i}\beta)^{y_{i}}\cdot\left[1-F(X_{i}\beta)\right]^{1-y_{i}}\\
\ln L & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}y_{i}\ln\left[F(X_{i}\beta)\right]+(1-y_{i})\ln\left[1-F(X_{i}\beta)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

